## Why turn-taking is important

Deciding when to take a turn is a crucial part of any speech-to-speech pipeline. Currently there are two approaches for building such pipelines – using a cascade of different models or picking a single unified <Term tip="The model listens and speaks at the same time, like a phone call, instead of taking strict turns like a walkie-talkie.">full-duplex</Term> model. Unified models, such as Moshi,<Cite id="moshi" /> are more natural conversationalists – they can handle speech overlaps and backchannels (“mm-hmm,” “yeah,” etc.), but they are difficult to train, requiring rare speech-to-speech data. The cascaded approach, on the other hand, is more flexible: you usually pick three models (an <Term tip="Automatic speech recognition: turns the user’s speech into text.">ASR</Term> model, an <Term tip="Large language model: reads the transcript and writes the text of the reply.">LLM</Term> and a <Term tip="Text-to-speech: turns the reply text back into audio.">TTS</Term>) and have a ready speech-to-speech pipeline in no time. Little to no training is required, and individual components can be replaced with newer ones as they become available. However, such cascaded approaches have two downsides: they usually suffer from higher latency (200 ms for Moshi compared to \~1 second of a cascaded pipeline<Cite id="chipchat" note="920 ms of latency was achieved on a highly-optimized pipeline on an Apple M2 Ultra." />) and they do not sound as natural in the conversation, since a lot of expressiveness is lost on the switch between modalities – the STT→LLM transition, for example, loses everything but text. Let's have a look at some examples:

<ConversationDemo
  demo="moshi"
  title="Unified (Moshi)"
  description="Note the rapid response time and the ability to handle backchannels."
/>

<ConversationDemo
  demo="pipecat"
  title="Cascaded ([Pipecat](https://github.com/pipecat-ai/pipecat))"
  description="Here, the latency is more substantial, but the model is considerably smarter."
/>

<p class="demo-notes">
  Both were run on an RTX 4090. The cascaded approach is running on <a href="https://huggingface.co/distil-whisper/distil-medium.en" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Whisper distil-medium.en</a>, <a href="https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-4-12B-it" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Gemma 4 12B</a> and <a href="https://github.com/kyutai-labs/pocket-tts" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Pocket TTS</a>. The cascaded approach provides more thoughtful answers, has a better TTS and recognizes words better, but lacks interactivity – notice the interruption on the “yeah” backchannel. For benchmarks on Moshi and how it fares against other models, visit <a href="https://artificialanalysis.ai/speech-to-speech" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Artificial Analysis</a>.
</p>



We are focused on perfecting the cascaded approach and making it as natural as possible, so let's talk a bit more about turn-taking latency in cascades. Nowadays, the pipeline can decide when to take a turn in three ways:

1. A separate turn-taking model is used, which fires a signal for the LLM to start speaking (for example, Smart Turn<Cite id="smart-turn" />).
2. The ASR itself emits the end-of-turn signal to the LLM (X2-Turn<Cite id="x2-turn" /> and Kyutai STT<Cite id="kyutai-stt" />).
3. ASR tokens are streamed to the LLM, and it decides on a fixed timer whether to start talking or keep silent – the DuplexCascade approach.<Cite id="duplexcascade" note="A decision on whether to start talking or not is made every 600 ms." />

<DuplexCascade />

And in all such pipelines it is paramount to balance accuracy and speed very carefully – take a turn too quickly while the user is thinking mid-sentence, and your user gets furious that they were interrupted mid-thought by something like “It looks like your response was cut off, what did you mean?” Take a turn too slowly and you get unnatural response times, again, ruining the user experience. Humans usually give replies in about 200 ms in a natural conversation.<Cite id="levinson-torreira" />

## Existing solutions

Now that we know what turn-taking is, let's take a look at existing solutions. A popular benchmark for turn-taking models is [TurnBench](https://turnbench.sesame.com/) – we picked the open-source entries, added other turn detectors and a plain silence timeout, and tested them all on our data:

**Turn-end detection on real Speakrail conversations**

| Model | Turn ends caught\* | Response delay<br />(typical / slowest 10%) | License |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| [VoxTurn](https://huggingface.co/AustinXiao/voxturn) | 94% | 0.08 s / 0.4 s | CC-BY-NC |
| [MaAI VAP](https://github.com/MaAI-Kyoto/MaAI) | 91% | 0.3 s / 1.0 s | MIT code; weights' license not stated |
| [Smart Turn v3.2](https://huggingface.co/pipecat-ai/smart-turn-v3) | 91% | 0.8 s / 1.2 s | BSD-2 |
| Silence timeout ([Silero VAD](https://github.com/snakers4/silero-vad)) | 89% | 2.2 s / 2.3 s | MIT |
| [X2-Turn](https://huggingface.co/x-square-robot/X2-Turn-4B-0812) | 74% | 1.4 s / 3.3 s | Apache 2.0 |
| [turn-1-mini](https://huggingface.co/p99lab/turn-1-mini) | 67% | 0.16 s / 1.2 s | Apache 2.0 |
| [LiveKit turn detector](https://huggingface.co/livekit/turn-detector) (text) | 66% | \~0.2 s\*\* | LiveKit model license |
| [Parakeet realtime EOU](https://huggingface.co/nvidia/parakeet_realtime_eou_120m-v1) | 58% | 2.2 s / 5.8 s | NVIDIA Open Model |
| [TEN Turn Detection](https://huggingface.co/TEN-framework/TEN_Turn_Detection) (text) | 50% | \~0.2 s\*\* | Apache 2.0 |

<p class="table-notes">
  * Share of finished turns the model responds to, while interrupting the user on at most 5% of mid-sentence pauses. Delay is measured from the user’s last word.<br />
  ** Text-only models were given an assumed 0.2 s for the transcript to arrive.
</p>

Out of those approaches, X2-Turn and Parakeet realtime EOU also output text tokens, while the others just output turn probabilities. We consider making the ASR model output turn probabilities alongside the text the most suitable approach in speech-to-speech because this simplifies the pipeline and doesn't need an external model.

## Our approach

### Turn-taking head

Our main idea was to attach a turn-taking head to an ASR model to decide when to release turns – this allows us to tap into the powerful acoustic and semantic features that the model was previously trained on. As our ASR, we chose the [Voxtral Realtime](https://huggingface.co/mistralai/Voxtral-Mini-4B-Realtime-2602)<Cite id="voxtral-realtime" /> model, since it shows the best performance among all open-source streaming models we found, both on [public tests](https://artificialanalysis.ai/speech-to-text/streaming) and on our benchmarks. Here are the results of our benchmarks:

**Errors on real Speakrail conversations**

| System | Meaning-changing errors per turn\* | How it ran |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **[Voxtral Realtime](https://huggingface.co/mistralai/Voxtral-Mini-4B-Realtime-2602)** | **5.3%** | live streaming, 480 ms delay |
| [Qwen3-ASR 1.7B](https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-ASR-1.7B) | 8.1% | offline |
| [Whisper large-v3](https://huggingface.co/openai/whisper-large-v3) | 12.0% | offline |
| [Parakeet TDT v3](https://huggingface.co/nvidia/parakeet-tdt-0.6b-v3) | 14.3% | offline |
| [Nemotron streaming-en 0.6B](https://huggingface.co/nvidia/nemotron-speech-streaming-en-0.6b) | 16.9% | streaming, 560 ms |
| [Kyutai STT 2.6B](https://huggingface.co/kyutai/stt-2.6b-en) | 21.5% | offline |
| [Nemotron 3.5 0.6B](https://huggingface.co/nvidia/nemotron-3.5-asr-streaming-0.6b) | 27.3% | streaming, 560 ms |
| [Kyutai STT 1B](https://huggingface.co/kyutai/stt-1b-en_fr) | 29.6% | offline |
| [Moonshine v2 Medium](https://huggingface.co/moonshine-ai/moonshine-streaming-medium) (245M) | 34.2% | offline, whole clips, batched |
| Gemma 4 audio ([E4B](https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-4-E4B-it) / [E2B](https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-4-E2B-it) / [12B](https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-4-12B-it)) | 40–44% | offline |

<p class="table-notes">
  * Meaning-changing errors per turn is an LLM-judged metric that inspects the transcripts and decides whether the meaning was altered or not. Claude Opus 5.5 was used as a judge.
</p>

Good, the ASR model is selected. Now we need to decide how exactly a turn-taking head should be attached to the model. **Our hypothesis: since the Voxtral model is capable of outputting punctuation, it should have an idea of <Term tip="The melody and rhythm of speech: pitch, loudness, pauses and timing. It is how a listener hears that a sentence is finished, or isn’t.">prosody</Term> and semantics, so, in theory, weights can be frozen and a turn-taking head can be attached directly onto the hidden states and trained to get information from them.** Keeping weights frozen would keep <Term tip="Word error rate: the share of words an ASR model gets wrong (swapped, dropped or added) against a reference transcript. Lower is better.">WER</Term> as is and ensure that the model doesn't catastrophically forget everything that it learned before – it was never trained to output turn-taking probabilities, after all. This approach is similar to the one used in X2-Turn.<Cite id="x2-turn" /> However, they do a full fine-tune run and extract turn-taking probabilities from the final layer, while we keep the weights frozen and rely on a separate head. Furthermore, such an approach allows us to use datasets that don't have text as output ([Smart Turn v3.1](https://huggingface.co/datasets/pipecat-ai/smart-turn-data-v3.1-train), for example). Another hypothesis is that the most useful information would lie somewhere in the middle of the decoder: in decoder-only language models, intermediate layers often carry richer representations than the last one, which specializes in predicting the next token.<Cite id="skean" note="Across decoder-only language models, intermediate layers consistently give stronger features for downstream tasks than the final layer." /> Layers closer to the input, in turn, stay closer to the acoustics.<Cite id="pasad" note="In wav2vec 2.0, information climbs from acoustic features in the lowest layers to phones, word identity and word meaning higher up." />

<DecoderTaps />

Let's check our hypotheses, starting with the place to tap into. We picked 2,000 clips from [Smart Turn](https://huggingface.co/datasets/pipecat-ai/smart-turn-data-v3.1-train) at random, saved hidden states from multiple layers and then trained small linear probes that output simple complete / incomplete probabilities. Complete probability means that the phrase is complete and that we can ask the LLM to "say" something, and incomplete means that the user has probably paused mid-sentence to think. Here are the results of this experiment:

**Where the turn-taking information lives in Voxtral's decoder**

| Decoder layer (of 26) | Accuracy, complete vs incomplete\* |
| --- | --- |
| 0 (first) | 92.5% |
| 4 | 95.3% |
| 11 (middle) | **96.4%** |
| 17 | 96.1% |
| Output (last layer, after the final norm) | 93.5% |

<p class="table-notes">
  * A linear probe per layer, trained on 1,603 clips and tested on the 397 held-out clips. Accuracy is averaged over 0.5–1 s after the user stops speaking, which is when Voxtral emits the last word.
</p>

Our hypothesis held up: the most useful information sits in the middle layers. We expected a bigger gap, honestly. Still, layer 11 came out on top, so we chose it as the input for the turn-taking head.

To choose the classifier, we took a 19,700-clip subset from the [Smart Turn v3.1 training set](https://huggingface.co/datasets/pipecat-ai/smart-turn-data-v3.1-train). We ran those clips through a frozen Voxtral Realtime model using the transformers package and saved the hidden states of layer 11 for the frames around the end of each utterance. We also did a LoRA tune on the last layers for comparison. Here is the comparison table on the cached hidden states:

**Candidates we tried**

| Head | Size | Reads | F1 complete / incomplete / backchannel | False fires on incomplete |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Linear probe | 15k | layer 11 | 0.951 / 0.948 / 0.928 | 5.7% |
| MLP, 768 hidden | 2.4M | layer 11 | 0.963 / 0.963 / 0.944 | 5.2% |
| GRU, 512 | 3.2M | layer 11 | 0.972 / 0.971 / 0.959 | 2.8% |
| LoRA rank 16 on Voxtral's top layers 18–25, plus a linear head | 2.9M | layer 17 | 0.964 / 0.964 / 0.949 | 3.4% |

All classifiers perform similarly on a held-out part of the [Smart Turn](https://huggingface.co/datasets/pipecat-ai/smart-turn-data-v3.1-train) dataset. On the [CANDOR](https://www.betterup.com/research/candor-research) dataset, however, the MLP was substantially better at deciding when the turn is over, which we consider more important than backchannel accuracy:

**The same heads on CANDOR, different domain**

| Head | CANDOR turn ends caught (119) | Typical delay | Cut in on mid-turn pauses | Took the floor on a backchannel |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Linear, layer 11 | 22% | 0.23 s | 10% | 34% |
| **MLP, layer 11** | **57%** | **0.26 s** | 19% | 31% |
| GRU, layer 11 | 37% | 0.14 s | 13% | 2% |
| LoRA, top layers | 25% | 0.15 s | 10% | 0% |

So, we decided to pick the layer-11 MLP, and selected the entire [Smart Turn 3.1 train set](https://huggingface.co/datasets/pipecat-ai/smart-turn-data-v3.1-train) and a 100-hour [OpenYap](https://huggingface.co/datasets/TheAgenticDataCompany/open-yap-1k) subset for training.
We ran the model on an RTX 4090, collecting the layer-11 activations, and then trained the MLP on those activations. The backchannel performance also improved, since the [OpenYap](https://huggingface.co/datasets/TheAgenticDataCompany/open-yap-1k) dataset contained more backchannel samples than the [Smart Turn](https://huggingface.co/datasets/pipecat-ai/smart-turn-data-v3.1-train) dataset.

### Speeding up Voxtral

Great, the LLM now knows when to take a turn, but at the time of the turn release the Voxtral model hasn't produced all the words yet. Voxtral Realtime has a delay setting<Cite id="voxtral-realtime" note="The target delay can be anything from 80 ms to 2.4 s, in 80 ms steps, and is chosen at inference time." /> that controls how far behind the audio the words are emitted. The optimal value is 480 ms,<Cite id="voxtral-realtime" note="Average WER on English speech: 9.95% at a 240 ms delay, 8.47% at 480 ms, 7.94% at 960 ms. At 480 ms the model approaches the accuracy of Scribe v2 Realtime." /> but Voxtral itself trails the actual end of turn by another ~150 ms, raising the total delay to ~630 ms. However, we can improve latency using the turn-taking head we just trained – it outputs the end-of-turn probabilities every Voxtral frame (that is, every 80 ms), and it doesn't depend on the delay setting. **This means that, knowing that a turn is over, we can push empty audio chunks and force a faster-than-real-time computation to cut down the end-of-turn latency – we call this “peek.”** The RTX 4090 running the Voxtral model through the [audio.cpp](https://github.com/0xShug0/audio.cpp) backend lets us run the model 6 times faster than real time, which means that we can theoretically cut end-of-word latency from 480 ms to just 80 ms. The reader might recognize this idea, and indeed a similar approach can be seen in [Unmute](https://github.com/kyutai-labs/unmute) by Kyutai.<Cite id="kyutai-stt" note="Kyutai calls it the “flush trick”: when Unmute detects the end of speech, it asks the speech-to-text server to process the audio already sent as fast as it can, faster than real time." /> The difference between those two approaches is that Unmute starts "looking into the future" when the model outputs that the turn is over, while we do that when the turn head is not sure yet – this saves latency for some extra compute. Let's have a look at some charts – this concept is easier to explain using pictures rather than words:

<SpeedupGantt />

But we can make <Term tip="Time to first audio: how long after you stop talking the first sound of the reply starts playing.">TTFA</Term> even faster by trying to anticipate when the turn will be over, Endpoint Anticipation style.<Cite id="endpoint-anticipation" note="Integrated into Unmute with a 960 ms horizon, average latency drops from 1195 ms to 690 ms, at the cost of 28.4% more speculative computation." /> The Endpoint Anticipation authors claim that their approach achieves a reduction of 505 ms on average response latency at a 960 ms prediction horizon. In practice, we didn't find such long horizons useful. When we gave Gemma 4 12B the [Smart Turn](https://huggingface.co/datasets/pipecat-ai/smart-turn-data-v3.1-train) requests cut 960 ms before the end, it answered wrongly 54% of the time. Taking that into account, we tried shorter horizons instead. Since Voxtral outputs the ends of words 100–200 ms after they actually happened, we can try to rely on acoustic information in the decoder and start the peek earlier. Hence, we added 3 more sigmoid outputs to our turn-taking head that output probabilities that the turn is over within the next 80 ms, 160 ms and 240 ms. We picked those values because a peek further than 240 ms ahead gets the user’s final words right less than 22% of the time. After training the model, we needed to pick a trigger point, and after experimenting with the thresholds, we decided to use the 160 ms output at a 0.7 probability. This yielded 76% of correct final words. Let's now have a look at the Gantt chart with all the optimizations described above:

<SpeedupGantt show={[4]} caption={false} />



## Results

First and foremost, thanks to frozen weights, the output of the model is unchanged – while using audio.cpp, the Speakrail and base Voxtral returned bit-identical outputs. The head itself costs just 0.02 ms on audio.cpp and consists of 2.4M parameters. Standalone detectors are larger (for example, Smart Turn v3 at ~8M and turn-1-mini at 6.1M) and still need their own ASR next to them. Let's have a look at turn-taking detection accuracy (same table as in [Existing solutions](#existing-solutions)):

**Turn-end detection on real Speakrail conversations**

| Model | Turn ends caught\* | Response delay<br />(typical / slowest 10%) | License |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **[Speakrail Voxtral](https://huggingface.co/speakrail/speakrail-voxtral-realtime-2602-turnhead-2611)** | 96% | 0.08 s / 0.3 s | Apache 2.0 |
| [VoxTurn](https://huggingface.co/AustinXiao/voxturn) | 94% | 0.08 s / 0.4 s | CC-BY-NC |
| [MaAI VAP](https://github.com/MaAI-Kyoto/MaAI) | 91% | 0.3 s / 1.0 s | MIT code; weights' license not stated |
| [Smart Turn v3.2](https://huggingface.co/pipecat-ai/smart-turn-v3)<Cite id="smart-turn" /> | 91% | 0.8 s / 1.2 s | BSD-2 |
| Silence timeout ([Silero VAD](https://github.com/snakers4/silero-vad)) | 89% | 2.2 s / 2.3 s | MIT |
| [X2-Turn](https://huggingface.co/x-square-robot/X2-Turn-4B-0812) | 74% | 1.4 s / 3.3 s | Apache 2.0 |
| [turn-1-mini](https://huggingface.co/p99lab/turn-1-mini)<Cite id="turn-1-mini" /> | 67% | 0.16 s / 1.2 s | Apache 2.0 |
| [LiveKit turn detector](https://huggingface.co/livekit/turn-detector) (text) | 66% | \~0.2 s\*\* | LiveKit model license |
| [Parakeet realtime EOU](https://huggingface.co/nvidia/parakeet_realtime_eou_120m-v1) | 58% | 2.2 s / 5.8 s | NVIDIA Open Model |
| [TEN Turn Detection](https://huggingface.co/TEN-framework/TEN_Turn_Detection) (text) | 50% | \~0.2 s\*\* | Apache 2.0 |

<p class="table-notes">
  * Share of finished turns the model responds to, while interrupting the user on at most 5% of mid-sentence pauses. Delay is measured from the user’s last word.<br />
  ** Text-only models were given an assumed 0.2 s for the transcript to arrive.
</p>

The turn head lets the pipeline take turns 767 ms earlier than a naive approach, and comparing our approach to the Unmute flush functionality on the same models yields a 308 ms improvement. Let's have a look at the compute table:

**Compute cost relative to the naive approach**

| Setup | ASR compute | LLM decision calls | Reply generation + TTS |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| naive approach (chart 1) | 1.00× | 1.00× | 1.00× |
| Unmute-style flush (chart 2) | 1.04× | 1.00× | 1.00× |
| **"Peek" on a copy + speculate at P ≥ 0.5 (chart 3)** | **1.07×** | **1.52×** | **1.10×** |
| **+ anticipation at 160 ms (chart 4)** | **1.13×** | **1.81×** | **1.24×** |

The anticipation approach turned out to be quite expensive in compute, but mostly it trades idle GPU time for lower latency. With more concurrent clients there is no idle time left to trade, and anticipation can end up making latency worse.

## Limitations and future work

First of all, we could have used more data to train the model (e.g., using the [otoSpeech](https://huggingface.co/otoearth) datasets). Furthermore, we noticed that the turn-taking head behaves worse on person-to-person chit-chat dialogues, and better on assistant-like data. That might be because the [Smart Turn](https://huggingface.co/datasets/pipecat-ai/smart-turn-data-v3.1-train) dataset had substantially more clips than the [OpenYap](https://huggingface.co/datasets/TheAgenticDataCompany/open-yap-1k) dataset. Limitations of the anticipation head must also be noted, as such actions require significantly more compute, and such approaches can't be used on low-compute systems or in multi-user deployments. For example, the Speakrail package disables the anticipation on Macs, because there is not much compute that can be traded for latency: even the top 80-core M5 Ultra GPU peaks at an estimated 33 FP32 TFLOPS,<Cite id="m5-ultra" note="Apple publishes no FLOPS figures; this third-party estimate comes from the GPU’s 10,240 shaders at 1.62 GHz." /> against the RTX 4090’s 82.6.<Cite id="rtx-4090" note="Peak FP32 throughput without tensor cores. With tensor cores, FP16 with FP32 accumulate reaches 165.2 TFLOPS." /> We reckon that our turn-taking head approach should generalize to other frame-synchronous ASR, such as [Nemotron Streaming](https://huggingface.co/nvidia/nemotron-speech-streaming-en-0.6b). This still has to be checked, though: unlike Voxtral, Nemotron has no LLM decoder.

## Deployment
To test the turn-taking head in your environment, you can use our fork of the [audio.cpp](https://github.com/speakrail/audio.cpp/tree/turn-head-2611) repository together with the [HF repository](https://huggingface.co/speakrail/speakrail-voxtral-realtime-2602-turnhead-2611) for the latest trained turn-taking head checkpoint. To try it together with the rest of the pipeline, use [Speakrail](https://github.com/speakrail/speakrail) – our open-source full-duplex speech-to-speech pipeline. Feel free to leave pull requests and open issues there.
